# useAbstractCropper

The engine behind every cropper component. It creates the cropper instance, loads the image, measures the boundary, keeps the state in line with the settings, refreshes on window resize, and returns the `cropper` object that `bind:this` exposes on `Cropper`. Use it to build a cropper with your own markup, as the built-in `AbstractCropper` does.

## Signature

```ts
function useAbstractCropper<Extension extends SettingsExtension = {}>(
	props: () => AbstractCropperHookProps<ExtendedSettings<Extension>>
): {
	cropper: AbstractCropperRef<ExtendedSettings<Extension>>;
	refs: AbstractCropperRefs;
	readonly image: CropperImage | null;
};
```

## Parameters

`props` is a **getter** that returns the options. The hook calls it whenever it needs a value, so return your component's props directly from it and every change is picked up:

```ts
const { cropper, refs } = useAbstractCropper(() => ({ src, settings: { aspectRatio } }));
```

| Option                                                  | Type                                          | Default    | Description                                                                                                       |
| ------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- | ---------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `src`                                                   | `string \| null`                              |            | The image to load.                                                                                                |
| `settings`                                              | `CropperInstanceSettingsProp<Settings>`       | `{}`       | Core settings (`aspectRatio`, `sizeRestrictions`, `defaultSize`, …). Missing ones are filled in by the defaults. |
| `transitions`                                           | `boolean \| { timingFunction?, duration? }`   | `true`     | Animate programmatic changes. `true` means 350 ms `ease-in-out`.                                                  |
| `postProcess`                                           | `PostprocessFunction \| PostprocessFunction[]` |            | Functions that adjust the state after each operation.                                                             |
| `*Algorithm`                                            | core algorithm functions                      | core's     | Replace a default modifier, for example `moveCoordinatesAlgorithm`. See [Modifiers](/docs/concept/modifiers).     |
| `checkOrientation`                                      | `boolean`                                     | `true`     | Read the EXIF orientation of JPEG images.                                                                         |
| `crossOrigin`                                           | `'anonymous' \| 'use-credentials' \| boolean` | `true`     | The `crossorigin` mode for loading the image. `true` means `'anonymous'`.                                         |
| `canvas`                                                | `boolean`                                     | `true`     | The fallback for `crossOrigin`. As that defaults to `true`, it rarely matters here.                               |
| `unloadTime`                                            | `number`                                      | `500`      | When an image is replaced, wait this many milliseconds for the old one to fade out before showing the new one.    |
| `autoReconcileState`                                    | `boolean`                                     | `true`     | Reconcile the state automatically when the settings change. Read once, when the hook is created.                  |
| `onReady`                                               | `(cropper) => void`                           |            | A new image has loaded, the state has been reset for it, and the result has rendered.                             |
| `onError`                                               | `(cropper) => void`                           |            | The image failed to load.                                                                                         |
| `onUpdate`                                              | `(cropper) => void`                           |            | Anything changed: the state, transitions, interactions, or the loading status.                                    |
| `onChange`, `onMove`, `onResize`, `onTransformImage`, … | `(cropper) => void`                           |            | The core's callbacks, with the same meaning as on [`Cropper`](/docs/components/Cropper).                          |

## Return value

- **`cropper`**: the cropper interface, with every method `Cropper` exposes (`getCoordinates`, `getCanvas`, `setState`, `zoomImage`, …). Its getters are reactive. It is also what callbacks receive. The object is stable, so you can destructure it or pass it to child components.
- **`refs`**: three slots the hook needs filled by your markup. Bind them as shown in the example below:
  - `refs.boundary`: the `StretchableBoundary` instance (`bind:this`). Without it nothing is measured and no state is created.
  - `refs.canvas`: the `CropperCanvas` instance (`bind:this`), needed by `getCanvas()`.
  - `refs.image`: the loaded `<img>` element from `CropperBackgroundImage` or `CropperSource` (`bind:ref`), the source `getCanvas()` draws from.
- **`image`**: the `CropperImage` currently displayed, or `null`. This is a getter: read it as `abstract.image` where you need it. Destructuring it takes a one-time snapshot.

## When to call it

Call it once, synchronously, in the `<script>` of the component that renders the cropper. It sets up effects (image loading, window resize, auto-reconcile), which Svelte only allows during component initialisation. Nothing touches the DOM until the component mounts, so it is safe during server-side rendering.

## Example

A minimal cropper assembled from the service components:

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
	import {
		CropperBackgroundImage,
		CropperBackgroundWrapper,
		CropperCanvas,
		CropperWrapper,
		RectangleStencil,
		StretchableBoundary,
		useAbstractCropper
	} from 'svelte-advanced-cropper';

	let { src }: { src: string } = $props();

	const { cropper, refs } = useAbstractCropper(() => ({
		src,
		onReady: (cropper) => console.log('ready', cropper.getCoordinates())
	}));

	// Expose the same methods as Cropper to `bind:this`.
	export const { getCanvas, getCoordinates, setCoordinates, zoomImage } = cropper;
</script>

<CropperWrapper {cropper} class="advanced-cropper">
	<StretchableBoundary bind:this={refs.boundary} class="advanced-cropper__boundary">
		<CropperBackgroundWrapper {cropper} class="advanced-cropper__background-wrapper">
			{#if cropper.getState()}
				<CropperBackgroundImage
					{cropper}
					class="advanced-cropper__background"
					bind:ref={refs.image}
				/>
			{/if}
			<RectangleStencil {cropper} />
		</CropperBackgroundWrapper>
		<CropperCanvas bind:this={refs.canvas} />
	</StretchableBoundary>
</CropperWrapper>
```

<Admonition type="note">

`refs.image` is typed `HTMLImageElement | HTMLCanvasElement | null`, the same as the `ref` prop of `CropperBackgroundImage` and `CropperSource`, so `bind:ref={refs.image}` type-checks. A custom background that draws into a canvas can bind its canvas the same way.

</Admonition>

## Differences from React

- `props` is a getter in both libraries, but in Svelte it reads your props directly, so the core always sees current values. React's version could lag one render behind.
- `refs` are plain bindable fields, not `RefObject`s. Use `bind:this` and `bind:ref` instead of `ref={refs.boundary}`.
- `image` is a getter property rather than a value captured at render time.
- `cropper` keeps the same identity for the life of the component. In React it was a new object on every render.
- `onReady` fires after the reset state has rendered, so `getState()` and `getCanvas()` already work inside it, even for the first image.
- `cropper.reset()` resolves once the state has been reset, not before.
- `getImage()`, `isLoading()` and `isLoaded()` return live values.
